ogy's real-world performance gap between "laboratory-measurable diffusion exists" and "functionally blocks vapor during use" is basically the gap between the marketing claim and your lived experience. You've essentially reasoned your way to the same conclusion that outdoor gear reviewers and materials engineers acknowledge more quietly: breathable-waterproof fabrics are a matter of degree, not a real solution, and that degree is often small enough to not matter once you start sweating.
